/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A proton and electron carry equal but opposite elementary charges":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A proton and an electron carry the same magnitude of charge, except in opposite directions. This magnitude of charge is $e$, or an elementary charge, which is roughly $1.602 \\times 10^{-19} C$.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important because despite their mass differences, a proton and an electron carry the same amount of charge.</p><h4>Graphical Demonstration</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Charges refer to different particles depending on the context\">Charges refer to different particles depending on the context</a><ul><li>Disambiguates which particles count as “charges” in a given problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI's Effect on Employment (Unfinished)":"<h3>Suppose AI Reduces the Number of People Needed to Perform a Large Fraction of Jobs, Threatening to Cause High Unemployment. What Measures Should Be Put in place to Limit Inequality in Pay (And, iDeally, also in hOurs wOrked)?</h3><ul><li>This is a tenuous counterfactual. But lets say that it is true at some point in the future.</li><li>The inequality of pay referred to seems to be the difference between the people with jobs and those without.</li><li>The real wages of the remaining human workers would increase as a result of AI automation, since that lowers the price of goods generally in the market, meaning that, even though the human workers’ productivity has not increased, they are paid more.</li><li>It seems to me that complete replacement of work by AI (that is, AI serving as a substitute and not a complement to human labor) is unlikely.</li><li>Perhaps with the exception of the lowest-level tasks, AI will merely lead to a simplification of tasks alongside humans, actually improving wages (through improving people’s productivity).</li></ul><h3>Meta</h3><h4>Relevant Notes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most profits from AI have gone to producers of hardware\">Most profits from AI have gone to producers of hardware</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capital&#x27;s share of income will rise in the future due to AI\">Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI will reduce wage inequality through &#x27;simplification&#x27;\">AI will reduce wage inequality through 'simplification'</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Minimum wage drives capital substitution\">Minimum wage drives capital substitution</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a></li></ul><h4>Per Claude Fable:</h4><p>This looks like the s  Economics Society competition, where this exact prompt appears as one of the topic options, with a September 1, 2026 deadline. Notably, the topic list features Christopher Pissarides (the search result references his book, Equilibrium Unemployment Theory, described as a canonical reference in the economics of unemployment), and he is well known for advocating shorter work weeks in response to automation. That tells you something about what the question-setter cares about. Now, your core worry rests on a mistaken premise, so let me push back on it directly: originality in an essay competition is not a novel thesis backed by new data. Nobody expects that from you, and judges know every entrant is drawing on the same literature. Originality here means three things: an original diagnosis, an original combination and prioritization of policies, and original stress-testing of your own position. You can adopt building blocks from Pissarides, Autor, Korinek, whoever, and still write something no one else will write, because the argument architecture is yours.</p><p>Concretely, here is how I would approach it:</p><ol><li>Diagnose before you prescribe. Most entrants will jump straight to a policy shopping list: UBI, retraining, robot taxes. Weak essays list policies; strong essays derive them. Start by asking why inequality rises in this scenario. The mechanism is roughly that AI shifts income from labor to capital: if output is $Y = F(K, L)$ and AI substitutes for labor, the labor share $\\frac{wL}{Y}$ falls, and since capital ownership is far more concentrated than labor endowments, inequality rises even if total output booms. Once you frame it this way, policies sort themselves: wage subsidies treat symptoms, while broadening capital ownership (sovereign wealth funds, employee ownership) or taxing capital income treats the mechanism. That derivation is where you look smart.</li><li>Exploit the clause everyone will ignore. The prompt explicitly asks about inequality in hours worked, and most entrants will treat that as decoration. It is actually the interesting part. A useful reframing: economist Alex Tabarrok recently argued that 40% unemployment and a 3-day work week are, to a first approximation, the same thing, since 60% of people employed full-time equals 100% of people employed at 60% of the hours, and the difference between catastrophe and wonderland comes down to how society distributes the gains. An essay built around the claim that the unemployment problem is an hours-distribution problem, and that policy should convert involuntary unemployment for some into voluntary leisure for all (work-sharing, hours regulation, overtime penalties, portable benefits decoupled from full-time status), would be distinctive and directly responsive to the prompt. There is even empirical grounding for caring about this: research finds that higher economic inequality itself increases working time and worsens work-life balance, operating through status anxiety and competitiveness, which gives you a nice feedback loop to discuss (inequality distorts hours, and maldistributed hours worsen inequality).</li><li>Commit to a thesis and attack it yourself. Do not survey five policies neutrally. Pick a position, e.g. “work-sharing plus capital diffusion beats UBI,” then spend a third of the essay on the strongest objections: lump-of-labor fallacy accusations against work-sharing, incentive effects of capital taxation, political feasibility. An essay that anticipates and answers the best counterargument reads as original even when every component is borrowed.</li><li>Interrogate the prompt’s premises, briefly. The prompt assumes AI displacement leads to unemployment rather than reallocation (history suggests reallocation has usually won so far), and assumes hours inequality is bad (someone voluntarily working 20 hours is different from someone rationed to 20 hours). One sharp paragraph noting these assumptions, then proceeding conditionally, signals rigor without derailing the essay.</li></ol><p>One honest warning: do not fake a formal model you cannot defend. A single well-deployed equation like the labor share point above is fine; a page of half-understood algebra will hurt you more than plain prose. Your comparative advantage at this level is clarity and argumentative discipline, not technique.</p><h3>Outline (1500 Words)</h3><h4>Introduction (~150)</h4><h4>How Inequality Happens (~400)</h4><p>There are multiple theories that seek a causal basis for technological unemployment</p><ul><li>Traditional theories (factor-augmenting models): these theories are essentially based on the assumption that productivity increases uniformly vis-a-vis factors of production (e.g., skilled vs. Unskilled labor).</li><li>Task-based approach: argues that the aforementioned theories are over-simplifications which ignore the actual nature of most innovations, which improve productivity for specific tasks and not for factors.</li></ul><p>For the second idea, the main point is just that tasks are automated faster than they are reinstated. There is also a potential explanation for persistent unemployment, which is the idea of efficiency wages, which is the idea that employers actually typically pay employees above the market-clearing wage in order to maintain morale and keep workers from reducing their productivity during work (shirking). However, combined with AI automation, this could lead to a situation where there is constantly an over-supply of labor given the high wages, despite firms hiring to their maximum.</p><p>It seems that “so-so” productivity increases have been far more common than brilliant technologies that completely reshape economies. It is mostly technologies that produce “so-so” productivity increases that have been causing unemployment.</p><ul><li>But it seems to be that it could be the case that these “so-so” improvements are riding on the coat-tails of new GPTs, and since AI is one such new GPT, across the board, it could have massive effects on productivity. </li><li>I’m not sure how these productivity improvements have been measured (specifically the 3.5 statistic). Look more into this.</li></ul><h4>Some Policy Proposals (~300)</h4><ul><li>Reduce/lower the work week?<ul><li>There is evidence to suggest that more free-time for the worker could potentially improve productivity or something?</li><li>There probably is diminishing marginal returns for continuous working hours. So maybe a better idea would be to retain the 5 day work week, but have shorter hours?<ul><li>What is the empirical research on this?</li></ul></li><li></li></ul></li></ul><h4>Why X is the Best Option (~500)</h4><h4>Conclusion (~150)</h4><h4>Bibliography</h4>","Currency devaluation causes unemployment because capital holders withdraw from the real economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>During times of monetary contraction, capital holders withdraw from the real economy since they are afraid of inflation eating up interests. As a result, interest rates rise, borrowing becomes difficult, and the economy slows.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a downside of currency devaluation, since it eats at economics growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>High inflation raises interest rates due to the fisher effect, and high interest rates leads to lower investment since people are less willing to borrow money when it is more expensive to do so. Less investment means that there is less growth in TFP, which means that economic growth may potentially slow.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>High inflation is a circumstance of high economics uncertainty.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that fair equality of opportunity has to be fully satisfied before any consideration of the difference principle.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important in understanding the way that the second principle of justice is to be implemented.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This rules out actions such as undermining individuals’ rights to have the ability to hold any position that they would be qualified through the justification that such a case would not be able to benefit the worst-off. Rawls specifically offers three arguments in favor of this claim:</p><ol><li>Once a certain threshold of material sufficiency is met, people come to recognize that their fundamental interests—particularly in self-respect and freedom of conscience—cannot be satisfied through additional economic gains, because these interests are not fungible with economic ones.<ul><li>This means that under economically stressful situations, economic welfare can be placed as a higher priority than protecting equal liberties.</li></ul></li><li>No one behind the veil of ignorance would be willing to gamble their freedom of conscience and freedom of thought for economic gains.</li><li>A serial ordering allows for inequalities in the socio-economic sphere to generate overall benefits while ensuring that individuals have sufficient primary goods.<ul><li>This relates to the idea of self-respect. It is impossible to secure self-respect through material means, since they are zero-sum, but equal basic liberties can indeed be secured for all. Grounding self-respect in this means that all can enjoy self-respect, and given this, people in the original position would never accept less than equal basic liberties since it would mean undermining the most important primary good, that of self-respect.</li></ul></li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a><ul><li>This then means that pure procedural justice is lexically before the difference principle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>Puts this in a larger context.</li></ul></li></ul>","Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>After the Revolutions of 1848, as well as the many revolutions that have occurred both in the colonies and in Europe (1832, for instance), governments shifted their strategy from repression to reform in order to prevent further revolutions in the future.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a change in the way that state institutions have approached the issue of how to maintain, shifting from a strategy of repression to reform.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Politicians (people like Disraeli and Bismarck) created social programs and expanded the right to vote in order to keep the population happy, draining the desire for revolution. It was not entirely the work of politicians, however, as food prices were dropping at the time and people standards of living gradually increased, meaning that they had more to lose in a rebellion. Nationalism also gained prominence in the period, something utilized by politicians in order to ensure that their populace stays loyal to the state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms\">The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms</a><ul><li>This is something that stopped occurring as a result of the reforms that occured.</li></ul></li></ul>","Goods of the soul are most valuable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A pursuit for what is good the soul, above what is good for the body or external goods, should be prioritized.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Aristotle’s argument for why one should be virtuous.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are a few arguments for this:</p><ol><li>This is the only way to acquire virtue; goods of the body or external goods cannot bring about virtue.</li><li>External things have a limit, so an excess of them is either harmful or neutral. Goods of the soul is the opposite, whereby the more of it you have, the better.</li><li>All the other goods serve the good of the soul.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should desire less\">We should desire less</a><ul><li>It seems that Taoism almost as an entirely opposite claim regarding that one ought to do.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice is an individual’s moral right":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mill’s ultimate definition of justice is that it is someone’s moral right, and it represents the rules of conduct applicable to all and intended for the good of all. Justice is a perfect duty.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Mill’s definition of justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice is essentially derived from the natural desire for retaliation when some social harm has been done. Importantly, the harm must be social (involving the general interest), or else, the retaliation is no longer a form of justice. Injustice occurs when:</p><ol><li>Someone is deprived of their liberty, property, or any other such lawful thing.</li><li>Taking something away from someone where that thing is a moral right.</li><li>One does not obtain what they deserve.</li><li>One breaks faith (a promise).</li><li>One is partial when one ought not to be.</li></ol><p>When there are conflicting claims of justice, utilitarian calculus is used to resolve the tension. For instance, if we must steal in order to save a life, the utilitarian calculus would allow for the stealing in order to preserve someone’s life.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having\">A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having</a><ul><li>What a right is.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Law is not constitutive of justice\">Law is not constitutive of justice</a><ul><li>The disconnect between the law and justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Maoism advocates for a 'bottom-up' revolution, while Leninism argues for a 'top-down' revolution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since there was a distinct lack of industrial workers in China that concentrated in working areas, China needed the revolution to spring from the people in the countryside, thus creating a “bottom-up” revolution that is counter to Marx’s original intentions. Importantly, administration remains “top-down.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a key difference between the Maoists and Marxist-Leninists.</p><h4>References</h4>","Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Part of the reason that the Mongols were so successful may have been that due to the warming weather at the time, the supply of available horses increased.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds complexity to the perspective that it was wholly the Mongols themselves, rather than their environment, that facilitated their success.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere\">Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere</a><ul><li>The other climatic explanation, running the opposite way: harshness produced the adaptability, warmth produced the horses.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies\">Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies</a><ul><li>The political precondition. Extra horses only matter once the tribes are pointed outward.</li></ul></li></ul>","One receives external moral truths":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That one’s sense of morality is something ultimately derived from external truths in the world, and not a result of one’s internal, natural, nature.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the position of the School of Principle of the Neo-Confucianists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>They argued that the mind, as with all other individual things, is the embodiment of <em>li</em> with <em>qi</em>, where <em>li</em> is the moral truths that exist in the world, independent of the mind, and <em>qi</em> is the substance that is required in order for the <em>li</em> and the <em>qi</em> to be bound together and create the mind. In this interpretation, the four constant virtues are of <em>li</em> and the four beginnings are of the operations of the mind. This theory also explains the nature of evil/bad/imperfect things in the world. What is bad is simple <em>qi</em> that does not align with the <em>li</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral truths arise internally and naturally\">Moral truths arise internally and naturally</a><ul><li>This is the theory subscribed to by the opposite school of thought, the School of Mind.</li></ul></li></ul>","One should strive to overcome emotion with reason":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that one’s emotions can be overcome with the use of reason, since many of the things that we feel strongly about, if reasoned correctly, would reduce our feelings towards it, keeping us more serene and in line with the Tao.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a trait that the Taoist sage embodies, and therefore, people should strive for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Any (negative) emotion can essentially be rationalized away, since it is either the result of one’s own doing, in which case one must then just rationally learn from one’s mistake, or it was outside one’s control, meaning that one couldn’t have done anything anyways, so there is no reason to grieve about it. Once one is able to rationalize away one’s emotions, then one’s happiness is not dependent on outside factors, which is considered absolute happiness.</p><h4>References</h4>","Quantitative easing is not the same thing as open market operations":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Quantitative easing is a far more radical form of open market operations, as it involves the large scale purchase of long-term government treasuries. This is as opposed to open market operations, which sees the purchase of short-erm government treasures.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two are different, since while the first is used commonly to make large adjustments to macroeconomy, usually in the aftermath of a major recessions, the latter is used commonly to make small changes to the money supply.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li></ul>","Suffering is good, if done correctly":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Dostoevsky argues that suffering can be good, and lead to a spiritual transformation, if it is done correctly. If done incorrectly, it can trap someone.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major theme that is explicated in <em>TBK</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Dostoevsky distinguishes between two forms of suffering:</p><ul><li><strong>Lamentation</strong>: “true suffering” that has a genuine cause that originates in the world. If one has faith and openness to the “web of connectedness,” lamentations can serve to purify us. However, if it is treated badly and allowed to fester, it can become the other form of suffering.</li><li><strong>Lacerations:</strong> suffering to feel morally superior to others, or because one feels guilt tripped into continuing to suffer. Dostoevsky considers this to be the wrong kind of suffering, and since it is centered on the self and pride, it leads to isolation.</li></ul><p>In order for suffering to “absolve one of sin,” one must go through a process of <strong>expiation,</strong> which means accepting the suffering and not avoiding it, for instance, through confession. Dimitri is the example that is used in TBK to show that at the end of suffering, there is light and joy. One just has to suffering in the right way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority\">Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority</a><ul><li>This is the idea of “lacerations” mentioned here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The ultimate purpose of the imperial examination system was not to find the best bureaucrats for the Chinese states, but to ensure that Chinese elites worked within the system, rather than against it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the ulterior reason for the imperial examination system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In essence, if a Chinese elite worked their lives in order to participate in the exams, they would not be able to counteract the state, since there is in fact a method by which the state accommodates them, though it is very difficult. That the system was not truly meritocratic can be seen through the fact that</p><ul><li>only the rich would have had the resources to sit for the exams, and </li><li>the exams often tested subjects that are not related to administrating.</li></ul><p>In fact, the end of the Chinese imperial state (1912) was closely proceeded by the abolishment of the imperial exams in 1905.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Who Was the Most Consequential Emperor in Chinese History?</p><p>Because all talent and ambition flowed into one state channel, <strong>little was left to build anything outside of the state</strong>. Yes, Keju raised China’s literacy, but <strong>no independent intelligentsia formed</strong>; nothing like a Chinese Royal Society or an independent Cambridge University came into being. <strong>Commerce was overshadowed, and rival ideas — Legalism, Daoism, Buddhism — were crowded out.</strong> Keju preempted alternative ideas and ideologies before they could organize.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>The stated justification for the exams, as against the political function argued here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>The class that integration actually produced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>This note argues against this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for enacting “Western neocolonialism” because the loans require neoliberal reforms in the form of austerity measures.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the criticism of the IMF that is often levied by the global South.</p><h4>References</h4>","The development of viable political institutions lags behind social and economic change":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The development of viable political institutions often comes after economic and social changes, since it is these changes that eventually lead to the creation of new political institutions. This means that, in the period before the new political institutions are created, there is naturally a period of instability.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the relationship between the development of political institutions and economic and social change.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When economic and social changes occur, they make drastic changes to the way that people live. Therefore, people demand political changes in order to accomodate for these changes, and often, this fuels political radicalism. An example of this is during the late 1800s and the early 1900s, during the second industrial revolution, when this lead to a wave of new radicalism in the form of Communism and Facism.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>But it seems that the rise of liberalism could also have been explained by this, specifically, by the industrial revolution? So the “political radicalism” is not necessarily bad, and thus this argument essentially still rests on the assumption of the supremacy of liberalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>But do institutions actually work to solve problems?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>This note seems to be in line with the linked note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>Here is another problem with instituting new institutions.</li></ul></li></ul>","The final stage of history is a positive one":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That the final stage of human history is a world in which positive thinking dominants and becomes the absolute principle of human reasoning.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the three stages argument put forth by Comte.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Positivism was formulated by Comte and it forms the final stage in his three stage progression:</p><ol><li>Theological/fictitious: knowledge is formed through superstition/supernatural agents, and seeks first and final causes.</li><li>Metaphysical: replaces the supernatural entities with abstract agents.</li><li>Positive: abandons the search for first and final causes, and replaces most knowledge acquisition tools with a priori and a posterori ones.</li></ol><p>The prior stages are necessary: the theological stage is that which first stimulates the human mind such that people are actually thinking about the relevant problems, and the metaphysical stage is necessary to smooth the process of the abstraction of our logical tools. The positive stage began with Descartes, Bacon, and other thinkers of the scientific revolution. Comte argues that eventually, such thinking would permeate the social sciences/humanities as well as the natural sciences.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>In order for positivism to triumph over the other schools of thought, Comte imagines using the structure of the Catholic church, with its separation of the spiritual (or in Comte’s case, the “spiritual” philosophy of positivism) and the political serving as the basic social order of society. A few problems with such a conception:</p><ul><li>It seems very unlikely that we are able to stripe the Catholic church merely of its spiritual elements and replace with positivism.</li><li>He critiqued the ideals of the church as being arbitrary and vague, though his own ideas can also be ascribed such labels.</li><li>His own ideas display a form of providence (namely in achieving a positivist world), which is intricately tied to Christianity.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action\">The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action</a><ul><li>The Catholic church is required in order for this final stage of history to be able to be executed.</li></ul></li></ul>","The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fiscal limit can be found using the standard debt dynamics equation ($\\Delta d \\approx (r - g)d - s$), which can be rearranged to produce $Fiscal \\ Limit = \\frac{s_{max}}{r-g}$.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to calculate the amount of acceptable debt that a government can take on.</p><h4>Derivation</h4><p>The most accurate version of this equation is actually$$ \\Delta d_{t} = (\\frac{1+r}{1+g})d_{t-1}-s $$ This comes from the debt and GDP equations, i.e., the following: $$ d_{t}=(1+r)d_{t-1}-s $$ $$ y_{t}=(1+g)<em>{t-1} $$ To get the change in the debt-to-GDP ratio in the following year then, we have to find $\\frac{d</em>{t}}{y_{t}}$ , which produces $$ \\Delta d_{t} = (\\frac{1+r}{1+g})d_{t-1}-s $$</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>A contributor to the debt, something that is discussed in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Predicted fiscal surplus lowers the risk of inflation\">Predicted fiscal surplus lowers the risk of inflation</a><ul><li>There are benefits to lower fiscal deficits (beyond what is covered in this note, )</li></ul></li></ul>","The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to ensure the stable growth of the economy, maintain low unemployment, and minimize inflation. If possible, it should also attempt to procure a positive trade balance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Macroeconomics is the study of macroeconomic policy, a branch of economics that has the stated objective. It is important to understand these objects in designing policy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs\">Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs</a></li></ul>","There is debate as to whether inequality is rising in the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is debate as to whether inequality is actually rising in the US, since different economists have used data differently to estimate inequality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This undermines the common statement that inequality is rapidly rising in the US.</p><h4>Different Measures</h4><p> Auden and Splinter have, for example, done research into inequality that contradicts the research that Piketty, Saez, and Zucman (PSZ) have done. One of the reasons for this difference is that the PSZ team assigns reported income proportionally, where the rich are assumed to have proportionately greater unreported income than the less-rich. Auden-Splinter, on the other hand, uses data from the IRS’s unreported income, which tracks mostly middle and lower income people’s unreported income (e.g., being paid in cash for a service), and uses that. Both methods have bias.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality has dropped globally, though increased within countries\">Inequality has dropped globally, though increased within countries</a><ul><li>For instance, it has increased in the US, though the degree to which it has increased is debatable.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>A potential cause of this inequality in the US, since there is so much innovation in the US?</li></ul></li></ul>","What is good does not need to be ‘proved’":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something that is good does not need to be proved that it is good, since it is intuitively obvious that thing is good. Reductively, something that is good is a means to something good.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This lies at the core of Mill’s utilitarian philosophy.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This seems rather weak to me. Additionally, it faces the Open Question Argument objection, which, although it has its own flaws, is regardless a notable problem for such a conception of the good.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good person is someone who has an above average moral character\">A good person is someone who has an above average moral character</a><ul><li>Related discussion of the good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>It is largely (only?) referring to moral goodness here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering is good, if done correctly\">Suffering is good, if done correctly</a><ul><li>Is this the case?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good\">Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good</a><ul><li>So goodness can be debated, which means that to some level, goodness as intuition differs between people. It seems crazy to say that goodness is entirely without a need for proof.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself\">One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself</a><ul><li>It seems more difficult for to account for this differentiation under this defintion.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
